Yes — that makes the idea materially stronger because the agent isn’t replacing the deterministic classifier. It’s a targeted escalation and learning layer around it.

The cost-effective architecture is essentially:

cheap deterministic classification first → escalate only ambiguous/novel cases to an agent → capture the agent’s reasoning/classification → identify repeatable patterns → upgrade deterministic rules → fewer cases require the agent over time.

That last part is especially compelling for Innovation Day. The agentic component doesn’t just classify hard URLs; it can help the system learn where its deterministic logic is weak.

I’d revise the submission to this:

Summary

Enterprise Technology Census with Agentic Classification & Workforce Intelligence

An automated system that analyzes enterprise application URLs to identify application types and technologies, using cost-effective deterministic classification for known patterns and targeted agentic analysis for ambiguous or complex endpoints. Results create an enterprise technology census that can inform modernization, technology adoption, and workforce planning while continuously improving the underlying classification capability.

Description

Large organizations have thousands of applications, but understanding what technologies are actually being used across the enterprise can be difficult. Technology inventories often depend on manually maintained records, surveys, or application teams reporting their own technology stacks. This makes it difficult to answer questions such as: What technologies are actually being used? Where are they concentrated? Which technologies are growing or declining? And what might that tell us about our engineering workforce and future training needs?

This innovation uses an existing inventory of enterprise application URLs as the starting point for an automated technology census.

The solution analyzes URLs associated with enterprise applications and classifies what each endpoint represents—for example, a user interface, API, documentation endpoint, authentication page, or other application surface. It then examines observable characteristics to identify technology signals and associates those findings back to the known application.

A key innovation is combining deterministic classification with targeted agentic analysis.

Most URLs that match established patterns can be processed quickly and inexpensively using deterministic scripts. URLs that cannot be confidently categorized, exhibit unfamiliar characteristics, or require deeper analysis can be placed into an exception queue for an AI agent to investigate during scheduled nightly or weekly processing.

This creates a cost-conscious tiered approach:

Enterprise URLs → Deterministic Classification → Confidence/Exception Detection → Targeted Agentic Analysis → Application Index → Enterprise Technology & Workforce Intelligence

Rather than using an AI model for every URL, agentic processing is reserved for cases where it provides additional value. This reduces unnecessary model usage while allowing the system to investigate edge cases beyond the current capabilities of the deterministic classifier.

Importantly, the agentic layer can also become a continuous improvement mechanism for the classifier itself. Repeated patterns discovered among previously unclassified URLs can be reviewed and incorporated into deterministic classification rules. Over time, known cases move into the faster, lower-cost path while agents remain focused on novel or difficult cases.

The resulting intelligence could help technology leadership measure actual technology adoption, identify legacy and emerging technology concentrations, track modernization trends, understand where technical skill sets may be concentrated, target workforce training and recruiting, and prioritize platform investments.

The longer-term vision is a continuously improving enterprise technology census that combines deterministic automation with selective agentic reasoning.

Instead of relying exclusively on “What technologies do we think we’re using?”, the enterprise gains another evidence source for answering:

“What are we actually running, where is it being used, how is it changing, and what does that imply for our technology and workforce strategy?”

The part I particularly like for your pitch is the feedback loop. I’d actually draw this if you’re allowed an attachment:

Classifier → High-confidence result → Index
Classifier → Low-confidence/unknown → Agent → Classification → Index
Agent discoveries → Reviewed reusable pattern → Classifier upgrade

That communicates something more sophisticated than “we added AI.” You’re proposing AI as an exception-handling and capability-discovery layer, while deterministic software handles what deterministic software is good at. That’s also much more defensible on cost, reliability, and explainability grounds.